    "<h2 style=\" border: 2px solid #AA5E7A; padding:5px; text-align:center\">Project Report</h2>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8be6557b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div style=\"border:1px solid #E5CD82; padding:20px\">\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Objective\n",
    "\n",
    "FicZon Inc. receives most of its leads through its website and other digital channels, and lead quality is currently categorised manually by the sales staff. The objective of this project is to (1) explore the data to understand sales effectiveness and (2) build a machine learning model that predicts whether a new lead is a **High Potential** or **Low Potential** lead, so the sales team can focus on the right leads.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Data Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset contains 7,422 records and 9 columns (`Created`, `Product_ID`, `Source`, `Mobile`, `EMAIL`, `Sales_Agent`, `Location`, `Delivery_Mode`, `Status`). Missing values are stored as blank strings: `Product_ID` (56), `Source` (17), `Sales_Agent` (23) and `Location` (56) have only a few, while `Mobile` is blank in 1,810 rows (24.4%). `EMAIL` has no blanks but contains many `#VALUE!` error entries. Only 2 duplicate rows were found, and the 80 `Open` leads were removed because they have no outcome yet, which leaves **7,340 records**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The `Status` values were grouped into the target `Target`: High Potential (Converted, Potential, Long Term, In Progress Positive) and Low Potential (Junk Lead, Not Responding, Just Enquiry, In Progress Negative, Lost). The final data has 2,849 High Potential and 4,491 Low Potential leads, so **38.8%** of leads are High Potential and the classes are only mildly imbalanced. A \"model\" that always predicts Low Potential would already be right about 61% of the time, so this is the baseline that real models have to beat.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key insights** (always read a rate together with the number of leads behind it)\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Source:** Customer Referral leads are among the best (about 89% High Potential). Small sources such as Existing Client (94%) and By Recommendation (88%) are also very strong but have few leads, while US Website leads are almost all Low Potential (about 1.5%).\n",
    "- **Location:** Bangalore (about 61%) and Chennai (about 51%) give the best leads among the larger locations, whereas \"Other Locations\" gives only about 17%. Malaysia and Europe show 100% but have very few leads, so that is not reliable.\n",
    "- **Sales Agent:** High Potential rate varies a lot between agents (for example Sales-Agent-9 about 63% and Sales-Agent-6 about 53% vs Sales-Agent-5 about 26%), which suggests lead handling and lead assignment both matter.\n",
    "- **Delivery Mode:** Mode-4 has by far the highest High Potential rate (about 67%), followed by Mode-2 (55%) and Mode-1 (51%), while Mode-5 is the lowest (about 25%).\n",
    "- **Contact details:** leads with a mobile number or a valid e-mail are more often High Potential than leads without them (see the `Has_Mobile` and `Email_Valid` tables above).\n",
    "- **Time:** The High Potential share is higher from September to November (about 44% to 46%) than from May to August (about 34% to 37%). April and November are partial months with fewer leads.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Data Preprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "- Duplicates were dropped, `Open` leads were removed, `Status` was converted to upper case and the target was created from it.\n",
    "- `Status` was removed from the features because it is the answer. `Created`, `Mobile`, `EMAIL` and `Month` were also dropped after features were taken from them.\n",
    "- From `Created`, four numeric features were built: `Hour`, `Weekday`, `Month_No` and `Lead_Age_Days` (days between the lead and the latest lead in the data).\n",
    "- From the masked contact columns, two 0/1 features were built: `Has_Mobile` (mobile number given) and `Email_Valid` (e-mail is not a `#VALUE!` error), because missing contact details can show a weaker lead.\n",
    "- Blank values in `Product_ID`, `Source`, `Sales_Agent` and `Location` were filled with \"Unknown\".\n",
    "- Sources with fewer than 100 leads were grouped as \"Other\", reducing `Source` from 26 values to 9.\n",
    "- Categorical columns were one-hot encoded (`drop_first=True`).\n",
    "- The data was split 80/20 with a stratified split (5,872 training and 1,468 test rows, `random_state=40`).\n",
    "- `StandardScaler` was fitted on the training data only and used for Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes, Linear SVM and KNN. The tree-based models use the unscaled features.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Model Building and Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "Seven classification algorithms were evaluated: Logistic Regression, Naive Bayes (Bernoulli), Linear SVM, KNN (k = 11), Decision Tree (max depth 8), Random Forest (51 trees, max depth 8) and Gradient Boosting (100 trees, learning rate 0.05, max depth 8). Class weights were set to \"balanced\" for Logistic Regression, Linear SVM, Decision Tree and Random Forest so that High Potential leads are not ignored. Models were compared on the 20% test set using accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score, where precision, recall and F1 are measured for the High Potential class since finding those leads is the goal. Because one test split can be lucky, the F1-score was also checked with 5-fold cross-validation on the training data."
   ]
  },
