   "source": [
    "## Challenges Faced and Techniques Used\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Creating the Target\n",
    "- Challenge: The data has 11 `Status` values but the project needs two categories, the values had a case mismatch (`CONVERTED` / `converted`), and `Open` leads have no outcome yet.\n",
    "- Technique: Converted `Status` to upper case, grouped the values into High and Low Potential and removed the 80 `Open` leads instead of calling them Low Potential.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Missing Values\n",
    "- Challenge: Missing values were stored as blank strings, so `isnull()` did not show them. `Mobile` was blank in 24.4% of rows and `EMAIL` had `#VALUE!` errors.\n",
    "- Technique: Found them with `(df == \"\")`, converted blanks to NaN and filled `Product_ID`, `Source`, `Sales_Agent` and `Location` with \"Unknown\". Turned the missing mobile and broken e-mail into the 0/1 features `Has_Mobile` and `Email_Valid` before dropping the raw columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Many Categories and Small Groups\n",
    "- Challenge: `Source` has 26 values and some have very few leads, and small groups can show 100% rates by chance.\n",
    "- Technique: Grouped sources with fewer than 100 leads into \"Other\" and showed the number of leads (n) next to every rate in the EDA.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. Data Leakage\n",
    "- Challenge: `Status` is directly used to create the target.\n",
    "- Technique: Dropped `Status` (and the helper column `Month`) from the features, and fitted the scaler on the training data only.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5. Precision and Recall Trade-off\n",
    "- Challenge: Accuracy alone does not show how many good leads are found, and High Potential is the smaller class.\n",
    "- Technique: Used balanced class weights where supported and compared precision, recall and F1-score for the High Potential class, against a baseline that always predicts Low Potential.\n",
    "\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
